00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:06.460 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  brien Keating's review of the big  picture on the origins of life meaning  and the universe itself by Bryan Keating  in physics today December 2016 at the  accepted rate of one picture per  thousand words  Sean Carroll's magnum opus the big  picture on the origins of life meaning  in the universe itself would be worth  about a hundred pictures Carol a  theoretical physicist at Caltech could  have easily entitled his book poetry for  physicists touching as it does on the  deepest cords of existence from  astrophysics to zoology we can begin as  carol does with definitions consider the  word universe religious interpretations  have it originating from the words 1 and  verse or from word as in the word or  verse said to be spoken by God in the  beginning the Roman philosopher  Lucretius one of the founders of  naturalism and a peripheral character in  the book is said to have coined the word  whose Latin translation means one role  to evoke everything enrolled or combined  into one this the sperrit view on the  seemingly innocuous word universe  perfectly capture the richness of the  big picture although there aren't many  new ideas in the big picture it shines  as a juxtaposition and synthesis of what  we physicists do no Carroll's book  contains the definitive treatment of a  wide range of subjects including quantum  entanglement bayes's theorem the Turing  test abiotic evolution the physically  relevant aspects of philosophy the  multiverse and of course the standard  model of physics the standard model is  the main focus of the tone which carol  calls the core theory a term coined by  Frank will check readers also receive  fresh insights into old standards such  as the entropic principle although  credulity might be stretched in  describing a 480 page book as lively  Carroll's humor shines in dry passages  such as those describing full employment  for philosophers or abducting God he is  adroit at quoting the secular Soren  Kierkegaard as the religious evangelist  Rick Warren he can mix Immanuel Kant  scat  coracle imperative with bill &amp; ted's own  imperative be excellent to each other  few authors so deftly mix the weighty  with the buoyant and that compels us to  read on from working the bowels of  particle accelerators to being a lab rat  inside of an MRI machine Carol  skillfully pulls off a synthesis of all  his intellectual peripatetic ISM Carol's  fundamental thesis is that we know  enough about the core theory to describe  all that can affect us we should not  fear the paranormal or the astrological  if they have any reality whatsoever they  must obey the laws of nature the core  theory new observations may require us  to expand or adapt the core theory of  course but as Carol puts it we know  enough to draw very powerful conclusions  about the limits of what we do know one  need only take a cursory look at the  many extant glowing reviews of the big  picture to be convinced it's worth  purchasing I feel duty-bound therefore  to focus on my few qualms with the book  some of the material in the big picture  including Carol's descriptions of  concepts like entropy and the error of  time seems to be more or less adapted  from his earlier books jettisoning some  of that material might have somewhat  reduced the books heft but retaining it  is forgivable Carol's descriptions are  worth reading twice the big picture also  takes the ambitious step of addressing  ethics alongside its physics  Carol is devoutly secular but not by - /  - his bias towards naturalism is evident  but he implores physicists philosophers  and theologians to engage in serious  respectful dialogue can one imagine a  humanist 10 commandments Carol takes a  crack at just that although that may  seem like a fool's errand Carol's  version delights and inspires we do not  agree with it we must at least grapple  with it while believers must confront  the question of theodicy the existence  of evil and a universe governed by a  good God humanists must confront the  existence of goodness in a world  governed by survival of the fittest how  does one explain performing acts of  self-sacrifice or giving alms to the  poor in a purely physical world so red  in tooth and claw to quote Alfred Lord  Tennyson Carol admits that there cannot  be an absolute morality without God but  that does not prevent him from trying to  establish ethics from scientific  principal's Carol sees humans is good  however he must grapple with the fact  that although individuals are good  humanity as a whole has a very mixed  record in the end  Carol's claim that human values can  emerge from patterns is unconvincing if  there is indeed a sociological arrow of  time towards goodness and caring as  asserted it is difficult to appraise the  initial conditions that impelled it to  be so religious texts aren't scientific  texts they're not intended to be read as  science and they haven't been cited for  scientific purposes since Giordano Bruno  time but can physicalism catalyzed by  the core theory actually provide  spiritual meaning at the end Carol  admits we cannot dispense with the  claims of religions in its own and it's  in their own domains so why bother  trying to extend physics into the  religious or ethical realm clearly  religion fills lacunae in people's lives  for the gaps that remain there is the  big picture a manual for the majestic  poetic existence of all that is paul  dirac famously quipped in science one  tries to tell people in such a way as to  be understood by everyone something that  no one ever knew before but in the case  of poetry it's the exact opposite  unification of physics escaped Dirac  Carroll's version of poetic naturalism  attempts to unify the physical with the  ethereal with its delightful blend of  evocative love pounds and four  dimensional integrals the big picture  offers a uniquely physical vision of  life's meaning this is poetry for Carol  it comes naturally  [Music]

